#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161732 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161923 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f80bc6ce-5b86-5883-a2bb-dbdc1e5503de" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability for a Chi-Square Random Variable Using the PDF"}] :question/id #uuid "531bdbe2-35a5-599f-8009-f65888b6f05d" :question/math-academy-id "e-16428" :question/problem "Given that $X\\sim \\chi^2(4)$, calculate $P\\left(X < 5\\right)$. Round your final answer to four decimal places.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following result:*\n\n$\\displaystyle \\int_0^{5} x e^{-x/2} \\, \\textrm dx \\approx 2.85081$\n\n*The probability density function $\\chi(k)$ is given by*\n\n${(1)/(2^{k/2}Γ(\\frac{k}{2}))x^{k/2 - 1}e^{-x/2},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ge 0 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$." :question/worked-solution "A chi-square random variable $X \\sim \\chi^2(k)$ with $k$ degrees of freedom has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{(1)/(2^{k/2}Γ(\\frac{k}{2}))x^{k/2 - 1}e^{-x/2},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ge 0 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nWe're given that $X\\sim \\chi^2(4)$. So, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{(1)/(4Γ(2))xe^{-x/2},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ge 0 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nRecalling that\n\n$$\n\\Gamma(n) = (n-1)\n$$\n\nfor any natural number\n\n$$\nn \\geq 1\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\Gamma(2) = 1! = 1\n$$\n\nSo, the probability distribution becomes\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{4}xe^{-x/2},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ge 0 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate\n\n$$\nP\\left(X < 5\\right)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X < 5) &= \\int_{0}^{5}f(x)dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{5}\\frac{1}{4}xe^{-x/2}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}\\int_{0}^{5}xe^{-x/2}dx \\\\\n&\\approx \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot 2.85081 \\\\\n&\\approx 0.7127\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161733 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161924 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c91d137-d318-5d98-9dc4-0b701a767b5e" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Percentage Points Table To Find a Chi-Square Probability"}] :question/id #uuid "1c328fe8-ba46-51f0-acb3-db3781a9d859" :question/math-academy-id "e-16429" :question/problem "The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X\\geq x) = p$, where $X\\sim \\chi^2(k)$. Given that the random variable $X \\sim \\chi^2(7)$, find the value of $x$ such that $P(X \\geq x) = 0.99$.\n\n| $k \\setminus p$ | $0.995$ | $0.990$ | $0.975$ | $0.950$ | $0.900$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $5$ | $\\,0.412\\,$ | $\\,0.554\\,$ | $\\,0.831\\,$ | $\\,1.145\\,$ | $\\,1.610\\,$ |\n| $6$ | $\\,0.676\\,$ | $\\,0.872\\,$ | $\\,1.237\\,$ | $\\,1.635\\,$ | $\\,2.204\\,$ |\n| $7$ | $\\,0.989\\,$ | $\\,1.239\\,$ | $\\,1.690\\,$ | $\\,2.167\\,$ | $\\,2.833\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Since $X \\sim \\chi^2(7)$, we focus on the row of the table that corresponds to $k=7$.\n\n| $k \\setminus p$ | $0.995$ | $0.990$ | $0.975$ | $0.950$ | $0.900$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $7$ | $\\,0.989\\,$ | $\\,1.239\\,$ | $\\,1.690\\,$ | $\\,2.167\\,$ | $\\,2.833\\,$ |\n\nFrom this row, we see that\n\n$$\nP(X \\geq 1.239) = 0.990\n$$\n\nTherefore, our answer is $x=1.239$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161734 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161925 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec19c717-37bc-53c3-b09e-21a2fd2b77fa" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Chi-Square Probability Using the Complement"}] :question/id #uuid "42104104-5483-5c90-be3f-dbe7b10cbff3" :question/math-academy-id "e-16430" :question/problem "The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X\\geq x) = p$, where $X\\sim \\chi^2(k)$. Given that $X \\sim \\chi^2(7)$, find the value of $x$ such that $P(X \\leq x) = 0.005$.\n\n| $k \\setminus p$ | $0.995$ | $0.990$ | $0.975$ | $0.950$ | $0.900$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $5$ | $\\,0.412\\,$ | $\\,0.554\\,$ | $\\,0.831\\,$ | $\\,1.145\\,$ | $\\,1.610\\,$ |\n| $6$ | $\\,0.676\\,$ | $\\,0.872\\,$ | $\\,1.237\\,$ | $\\,1.635\\,$ | $\\,2.204\\,$ |\n| $7$ | $\\,0.989\\,$ | $\\,1.239\\,$ | $\\,1.690\\,$ | $\\,2.167\\,$ | $\\,2.833\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "The table shows probabilities in the form $P(X > x)$. So first, we express the desired probability in this form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X > x) &= 1 - P(X \\le x) \\\\\n&= 1 - 0.005 \\\\\n&= 0.995\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $X \\sim \\chi^2(7)$, we focus on the row of the table that corresponds to $k=7$.\n\n| $k \\setminus p$ | $0.995$ | $0.990$ | $0.975$ | $0.950$ | $0.900$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $7$ | $\\,0.989\\,$ | $\\,1.239\\,$ | $\\,1.690\\,$ | $\\,2.167\\,$ | $\\,2.833\\,$ |\n\nFrom this row, we see that\n\n$$\nP(X > 0.989) = 0.995\n$$\n\nTherefore, our answer is $x=0.989$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161735 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161926 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a75e6c3-16e3-50ab-bd36-a2c83ba9288a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Probability Involving Sums of Squared Standard Normal Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "ab5d8857-fdb8-5f4f-85cc-ba9d9e480081" :question/math-academy-id "e-16432" :question/problem "Let the random variable $Y$ be defined as\n$Y = Z_1^2+Z_2^2+\\cdots + Z_{10}^2$\nwhere $Z_i\\sim N(0,1)$ for $1\\leq i\\leq 10$. Find the value $y$ that satisfies $P(Y\\leq y) = 0.99$.\n\n*Hint: The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X\\geq x) = p$, where $X\\sim \\chi^2(k)$.*\n\n| $k \\setminus p$ | $0.010$ | $0.050$ | $0.100$ | $0.150$ | $0.200$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $8$ | $\\,20.090\\,$ | $\\,15.507\\,$ | $\\,13.362\\,$ | $\\,12.027\\,$ | $\\,11.030\\,$ |\n| $9$ | $\\,21.666\\,$ | $\\,16.919\\,$ | $\\,14.684\\,$ | $\\,13.288\\,$ | $\\,12.242\\,$ |\n| $10$ | $\\,23.209\\,$ | $\\,18.307\\,$ | $\\,15.987\\,$ | $\\,14.534\\,$ | $\\,13.442\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "If $Z_i\\sim N(0,1)$ for\n\n$$\n1\\leq i \\leq k\n$$\n\nare independent normal random variables, then the sum\n$Z_1^2+Z_2^2+\\cdots + Z_{k}^2$\nfollows a $\\chi^2(k)$ distribution with $k$ degrees of freedom.\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\nY = Z_1^2+Z_2^2+\\cdots + Z_{10}^2 \\sim \\chi^2(10)\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the given probability in the form\n\n$$\nP(Y\\geq y){:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(Y \\le y) &= 1 - P(Y \\ge y) \\\\\n0.99 &= 1 - P(Y \\ge y) \\\\\nP(Y \\ge y) &= 0.01\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we wish to find the value $y$ such that\n\n$$\nP(Y\\geq y) = 0.01\n$$\n\nThis can be found from the given table:\n\n| $k \\setminus p$ | $0.010$ | $0.050$ | $0.100$ | $0.150$ | $0.200$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $8$ | $\\,20.090\\,$ | $\\,15.507\\,$ | $\\,13.362\\,$ | $\\,12.027\\,$ | $\\,11.030\\,$ |\n| $9$ | $\\,21.666\\,$ | $\\,16.919\\,$ | $\\,14.684\\,$ | $\\,13.288\\,$ | $\\,12.242\\,$ |\n| $10$ | $\\,\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}23.209}\\,$ | $\\,18.307\\,$ | $\\,15.987\\,$ | $\\,14.534\\,$ | $\\,13.442\\,$ |\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(Y\\geq 23.209) = 0.01\n$$\n\nwhich means\n\n$$\nP(Y\\leq 23.209) = 0.99\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value $y$ that satisfies\n\n$$\nP(Y\\leq y) = 0.99\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\ny=\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}23.209}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162238 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161924 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c91d137-d318-5d98-9dc4-0b701a767b5e" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Percentage Points Table To Find a Chi-Square Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e6f5ea9-b1e5-53dc-94ee-55cb87ceb89e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.115" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158631} {:answer/id #uuid "da02eab8-ed7f-5dab-b1ee-57eb6eabc16a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.297" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158632} {:answer/id #uuid "1e7af3fd-4452-5e37-a8ff-601eec6ff8de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.554" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158633} {:answer/id #uuid "4c0e55a4-179a-5313-9fb1-c335c2b9ce63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.412" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158634} {:answer/id #uuid "7127ac09-9a99-5bf7-96ac-2a04827eacc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.610" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158635}] :answer-field/id #uuid "fd00ac13-7639-5da6-828a-279c701cd404" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158630}] :question/id #uuid "e3878995-f0e7-5a70-8415-579b84758a63" :question/math-academy-id "q-105788" :question/problem "The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X \\ge x) = p$, where $X∼χ^{2}(k)$. Given that the random variable $X∼χ^{2}(5)$, find the value of $x$ such that $P(X \\ge x) = 0.99$.\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.995$ | $0.990$ | $0.975$ | $0.950$ | $0.900$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $3$ | $0.072$ | $0.115$ | $0.216$ | $0.352$ | $0.584$ |\n| $4$ | $0.207$ | $0.297$ | $0.484$ | $0.711$ | $1.064$ |\n| $5$ | $0.412$ | $0.554$ | $0.831$ | $1.145$ | $1.610$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162241 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161925 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec19c717-37bc-53c3-b09e-21a2fd2b77fa" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Chi-Square Probability Using the Complement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "acba8283-9370-5e8a-8629-c48cda23ad8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.825" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158649} {:answer/id #uuid "e78fb494-9343-52cc-bae6-91bf30fbfc15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.802" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158650} {:answer/id #uuid "ed92aa50-fed3-56f4-8351-dc2c198e8ade" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.325" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158651} {:answer/id #uuid "15d3173d-dc89-5f8a-9277-00d8a3c7fdb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.352" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158652} {:answer/id #uuid "2cca45eb-a458-5398-b3ce-cc5f6cdec82f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.305" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158653}] :answer-field/id #uuid "42f7a27e-becb-5e70-b581-496783b99c55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158648}] :question/id #uuid "8e86557c-9410-5c5f-92dc-391547fa52a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-108307" :question/problem "The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X \\ge x) = p$, where $X∼χ^{2}(k)$. Given that $X∼χ^{2}(9)$, find the value of $x$ such that $P(X \\le x) = 0.05$.\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.995$ | $0.990$ | $0.975$ | $0.950$ | $0.900$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $7$ | $0.989$ | $1.239$ | $1.690$ | $2.167$ | $2.833$ |\n| $8$ | $1.344$ | $1.646$ | $2.180$ | $2.733$ | $3.490$ |\n| $9$ | $1.735$ | $2.088$ | $2.700$ | $3.325$ | $4.168$ |"}]}
